# Expo / React Native App Rules

You are building Expo (`expo-router`) React Native apps. Prefer Expo SDK
APIs and existing project patterns over inventing new abstractions. These
are outcome-based rules — the experience described must hold, not just the
library named.

---

## 1. Navigation & Structure

- Use `expo-router` file-based routes (`app/`). Prefer `Link` /
  `router.push` / `router.replace` over raw React Navigation calls unless
  the project already does otherwise.
- Keep screens thin: reusable UI in `src/components`, hooks in
  `src/hooks`, API/data clients in `src/api` (or the project's equivalent).
- Respect the existing tab/stack layout. Do not invent a second
  navigation shell.

## 2. Layout & Safe Areas

- Use `react-native-safe-area-context` (`SafeAreaView` /
  `useSafeAreaInsets`) — never hardcode status-bar or notch padding.
- Account for home-indicator / tab-bar overlap on bottom CTAs and lists.
- Prefer `flex: 1` screen roots. Avoid fixed heights that break on small
  phones.

## 3. Keyboard Handling (non-negotiable)

Every screen with a `TextInput` must keep the focused field visible above
the keyboard automatically — this must never take a follow-up prompt to fix.

- Wrap the root layout (`app/_layout.tsx`) in `<KeyboardProvider>` from
  `react-native-keyboard-controller`. Add it if missing rather than
  building a screen-level workaround.
- Every screen/sheet with one or more inputs uses `KeyboardAwareScrollView`
  from that same library as its scroll container — not a bare
  `ScrollView`, and not the built-in `KeyboardAvoidingView`, which behaves
  inconsistently between iOS and Android.
- Never set `justifyContent: "center"` on a form's
  `contentContainerStyle` — it pins fields under the keyboard instead of
  scrolling them into view.
- Tapping outside an input dismisses the keyboard.
- The submit/primary CTA must stay reachable while the keyboard is open.
- For multi-field forms, add `KeyboardToolbar` so users can jump between
  fields with next/prev controls.
- Bottom sheets with inputs: pad using the library's offset/inset props,
  not guesswork.
- This requires a development build, not Expo Go — assume a dev build
  exists unless told otherwise.

## 4. Data Fetching, Caching & Instant Navigation

Data is fetched once, cached properly, and reused. Revisiting any
previously loaded screen — even after the app was fully closed and
reopened — must feel instant, with fresh data syncing quietly underneath.

- Use TanStack Query for all server-state. Never hand-roll fetching with
  `useEffect` + `useState`.
- Persist the query cache to disk via `@tanstack/react-query-persist-client`
  backed by `react-native-mmkv` — not `AsyncStorage`, which rehydrates too
  slowly to feel instant on cold start.
- Restore the persisted cache before the first screen renders (gate on a
  hydration flag / hold the splash screen) rather than after.
- Set a real `staleTime` per query based on how often that data actually
  changes. If cached data exists, render it immediately and refetch in
  the background — never block rendering to check for updates.
- Never refetch on every screen focus by default; scope refetch-on-focus
  to queries that are genuinely time-sensitive for that screen.
- Avoid duplicate fetches for the same resource across components.
- Local UI/app state that should survive restarts (last tab, filters,
  draft input, auth) goes through Zustand with MMKV-backed persistence —
  not React Context, not plain in-memory state.
- Never store secrets in the client bundle; use env vars via Expo config
  (`expo-constants` / EAS secrets) as the project already does.
- Handle auth expiry, offline/network errors, and empty API responses in
  the UI itself, not only in console logs.

## 5. Loading States

A loading spinner is the exception, not the default.

- Show a full-screen loader only the very first time a query has never
  been cached — if cached data exists, render it immediately regardless
  of staleness.
- Use skeleton placeholders instead of bare spinners when a genuine
  loading state is unavoidable.
- Keep previous data visible during a background refresh; pull-to-refresh
  must never clear existing content while it runs.
- Every data screen needs loading, empty, and error states — never a
  blank spinner forever or a silent failure.

## 6. Real-Time Updates

Where the backend supports it, screens stay live instead of re-fetching
on a loop.

- Use real-time subscriptions instead of polling when the backend allows it.
- Merge incoming updates into existing state rather than reloading the
  whole screen.
- Preserve scroll position and in-progress user interaction when new data
  arrives; don't interrupt what the user is doing.

## 7. Lists, Media & Performance

Assume every screen is judged against a smooth 60fps mobile experience.

- Use `FlatList` for small/simple lists (roughly under a few hundred
  items). For anything larger, or any list showing scroll jank or blank
  cells, use `FlashList` (`@shopify/flash-list`) — v2 requires the New
  Architecture, needs no size estimates, and runs in Expo Go on SDK 55+.
  Never render long lists with `ScrollView` + `.map()`.
- Virtualize feeds; avoid mounting every video/image at once, and pause
  off-screen media.
- Use `expo-image`, not the bare `Image` component, for loading/caching;
  size images appropriately — don't load full-res into small thumbnails.
- Memoize expensive components/values; never fetch data inside a render
  function.
- Use pagination or infinite scroll for large datasets instead of loading
  everything at once.

## 8. Platform Handling

- Use `Platform.OS` / `Platform.select` only when behavior genuinely
  differs (keyboard, shadows, status bar, file pickers, haptics).
- Prefer cross-platform Expo modules (`expo-image`, `expo-video`,
  `expo-secure-store`, `expo-haptics`, `expo-image-picker`, etc.) over
  custom native modules unless required.
- Mentally test both iOS and Android; call out platform-only APIs explicitly.

## 9. Styling & UX

- Match the project's existing theme tokens / design system — don't
  invent one-off colors or spacing if tokens already exist.
- Touch targets ≥ ~44pt. Disable double-submit on buttons while a request
  is in flight.
- Prefer native-feeling transitions already used in the app (sheets,
  fades) over introducing a new animation library.

## 10. Forms & Inputs

- Controlled inputs with clear, inline validation errors.
- Dismiss the keyboard on submit where appropriate; keep the primary CTA
  reachable while typing (see Section 3).
- Pickers/modals restore focus and scroll position sensibly when closed.

---

## Before Finishing Any Screen

1. Trace the full user path: open → load → act → success/error.
2. Confirm keyboard coverage on every new or edited input.
3. Confirm safe-area top and bottom on notched devices.
4. Confirm loading, empty, and error UI all exist.
5. Confirm revisiting the screen renders from cache instantly, with no
   avoidable spinner.
6. Don't leave TODO stubs for core UX.

### Default stack this project assumes
`expo-router` · `react-native-safe-area-context` ·
`react-native-keyboard-controller` · TanStack Query ·
`react-native-mmkv` · Zustand · FlashList (for large lists) ·
`expo-image` · real-time subscriptions over polling where supported.
